        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0950-382X, 1365-2958</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_91">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-citation-items=&quot;%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F9YL6TKGH%22%5D%2C%22itemData%22%3A%7B%22id%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F9YL6TKGH%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22article-journal%22%2C%22abstract%22%3A%22Bacterial%20cells%20activate%20stress-sensing%20and%20stress-mitigating%20pathways%20by%20employing%20a%20number%20of%20transcription%20regulators%2C%20some%20of%20which%20can%20bind%20directly%20to%20RNA%20polymerase%20to%20activate%20stress-specific%20response%20pathways.%20However%2C%20mutations%20in%20the%20RNA%20polymerase%20genes%20can%20accumulate%20under%20certain%20selection%20conditions%20and%20activate%20stress-mitigating%20pathways%20in%20a%20manner%20that%20is%20partly%20independent%20of%20pathway-specific%20regulators.%20In%20this%20study%2C%20we%20characterized%20a%20novel%20mutation%20in%20the%20rpoB%20gene%20that%20transforms%20RNA%20polymerase%20into%20a%20%E2%80%9Cstringent%E2%80%9D%20polymerase%20in%20the%20absence%20of%20one%20of%20the%20key%20stringent%20response%20(SR)%20activating%20factors%20(p)ppGpp%2C%20produced%20by%20the%20relA%20gene%20product.%20The%20mutant%20RNA%20polymerase%20allele%2C%20rpoB58%2C%20elevated%20thermotolerance%20and%20permitted%20growth%20without%20the%20key%20molecular%20chaperones%20(DnaKJ)%20and%20proteases%20(Lon%2C%20ClpP)%20at%20temperatures%20nonpermissive%20to%20cells%20expressing%20the%20wild%20type%20RNA%20polymerase%20genes.%20Remarkably%2C%20rpoB58%20also%20reversed%20the%20cell%20division%20defect%20of%20%CE%94dnaJ%20at%20a%20nonpermissive%20temperature%20but%20could%20not%20overcome%20its%20lambda%20phage-resistant%20phenotype.%20The%20rpoB58-mediated%20rescue%20of%20the%20%CE%94dnaKJ%20growth%20defect%20was%20partly%20reversed%20in%20the%20absence%20of%20DksA%2C%20a%20protein%20that%20acts%20synergistically%20with%20(p)ppGpp%20to%20transform%20RNA%20polymerase%20into%20a%20stringent%20state.%20The%20data%20suggest%20that%20pre-activated%20SR%20confers%20thermotolerance%20and%20chaperone%20independence%20in%20part%20by%20lowering%20protein%20synthesis.%22%2C%22container-title%22%3A%22Molecular%20Microbiology%22%2C%22DOI%22%3A%2210.1111%2Fmmi.70011%22%2C%22ISSN%22%3A%220950-382X%2C%201365-2958%22%2C%22issue%22%3A%224%22%2C%22journalAbbreviation%22%3A%22Molecular%20Microbiology%22%2C%22language%22%3A%22en%22%2C%22page%22%3A%22310-326%22%2C%22source%22%3A%22DOI.org%20(Crossref)%22%2C%22title%22%3A%22Characterization%20of%20a%20Novel%20%3Cspan%20style%3D%5C%22font-variant%3Asmall-caps%3B%5C%22%3ERNA%3C%2Fspan%3E%20Polymerase%20Mutant%20of%20%3Cspan%20style%3D%5C%22font-variant%3Asmall-caps%3B%5C%22%3E%20%3Ci%3EEscherichia%20coli%3C%2Fi%3E%20%3C%2Fspan%3E%20That%20Confers%20Thermotolerance%20and%20Chaperone%20Independence%22%2C%22title-short%22%3A%22Characterization%20of%20a%20Novel%20%3Cspan%20style%3D%5C%22font-variant%22%2C%22URL%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fonlinelibrary.wiley.com%2Fdoi%2F10.1111%2Fmmi.70011%22%2C%22volume%22%3A%22124%22%2C%22author%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Yeh%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Melody%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Kelly%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Keilen%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Misra%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Rajeev%22%7D%5D%2C%22accessed%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222026%22%2C2%2C5%5D%5D%7D%2C%22issued%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222025%22%2C10%5D%5D%7D%7D%7D%5D&quot; data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;rpoB mutations recur in heat-adapted populations because they convert RNA polymerase into a “stringent-like” state that lowers protein synthesis, stabilises transcription, and restores protein homeostasis under heat stress—thereby removing the main growth-limiting constraint at high temperature.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FJ3WRY5FY%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%22319%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A9%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B520.1670999999996%2C451.3480000000002%2C549.9337000000002%2C460.6180000000002%5D%2C%5B306.15070000000014%2C439.8460000000002%2C549.9436000000001%2C449.1160000000002%5D%2C%5B306.15070000000014%2C428.34400000000016%2C549.9337000000002%2C437.6140000000002%5D%2C%5B306.15070000000014%2C416.84200000000016%2C549.9580000000001%2C426.1120000000002%5D%2C%5B306.15070000000014%2C405.34000000000015%2C549.9103%2C414.6100000000002%5D%2C%5B306.15070000000014%2C393.83800000000014%2C549.9076%2C403.1080000000002%5D%2C%5B306.15070000000014%2C382.3360000000001%2C549.9373%2C391.60600000000017%5D%2C%5B306.15070000000014%2C370.8340000000001%2C549.9139000000004%2C380.10400000000016%5D%2C%5B306.15070000000014%2C359.3320000000001%2C549.9480999999996%2C368.60200000000015%5D%2C%5B306.15070000000014%2C347.8300000000001%2C549.9301000000005%2C357.10000000000014%5D%2C%5B306.15070000000026%2C336.3280000000001%2C351.5917000000002%2C345.5980000000001%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F9YL6TKGH%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%22319%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“The inhibition of E. coli growth at temperatures higher than 46°C is likely due to debilitating effects on cell structure–function, with a collapse in protein homeostasis being one of the major factors. Indeed, null mutations in genes central to the maintenance of protein homeostasis, such as those coding for molecular chaperones and proteases, significantly lower the maximum growth temperature and are classically linked to growth defects at temperatures between 37°C and 42°C (Paek and Walker 1987; Strauch et al. 1989; Sell et al. 1990; Ueguchi et al. 1995).”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F9YL6TKGH%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%22319%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Yeh et al., 2025, p. 319&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;This model describes how both (p)ppGpp-dependent and (p)ppGpp-independent pathways improve protein homeostasis by lowering protein synthesis and activating stress responses. In the (p)ppGpp-dependent pathway, starvation or constitutive RelA activation elevates (p)ppGpp, triggering the stringent response through RNA polymerase, which reduces macromolecular synthesis and stimulates stress-mitigation pathways. These effects secondarily enhance protein homeostasis. Similarly, direct slowing of protein synthesis—via chloramphenicol or growth in minimal medium—also improves protein homeostasis.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The (p)ppGpp-independent pathway involves rpoB mutations (e.g., rpoB58) that constitutively shift RNA polymerase into a stringent-like state, producing transcriptional outcomes similar to the stringent response without requiring (p)ppGpp. RNA-seq data show that this pathway likewise lowers protein synthesis and improves protein homeostasis.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;DksA is essential in both pathways, as loss of DksA severely limits the ability of rpoB58 to restore protein homeostasis, particularly in cells lacking chaperones. Experimental evidence shows that reducing protein synthesis—either pharmacologically or through slow growth—rescues growth defects at high temperatures, even without DnaKJ. Although elevated (p)ppGpp can contribute under some conditions, rpoB58-mediated restoration of protein homeostasis does not require (p)ppGpp.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Overall, the data support a model in which reduced protein synthesis rebalances protein homeostasis under stress, contributing to thermotolerance, with stringent-response factors such as DksA playing a central role.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.google.com/search?client=firefox-b-d&amp;amp;channel=entpr&amp;amp;q=Protein+synthesis&amp;amp;ved=2ahUKEwiOrZOKlsSSAxU5tVYBHdKCGMcQgK4QegQIARAC&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;Protein synthesis&lt;/a&gt; is the specific, regulated process of creating new proteins via translation of mRNA, whereas &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.google.com/search?client=firefox-b-d&amp;amp;channel=entpr&amp;amp;q=protein+homeostasis+%28proteostasis%29&amp;amp;ved=2ahUKEwiOrZOKlsSSAxU5tVYBHdKCGMcQgK4QegQIARAD&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;protein homeostasis (proteostasis)&lt;/a&gt; is the broader, holistic equilibrium maintaining the entire proteome through a balance of synthesis, folding, trafficking, and degradation. Synthesis produces proteins, while homeostasis ensures they remain functional and are removed when damaged.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;Protein homeostasis fails not just because folding capacity is low, but because protein synthesis is too high.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;rpoB58 converts RNA polymerase into a &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;stringent-like enzyme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;This:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;Lowers ribosome production&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;Slows translation&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;Reduces misfolding pressure&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;As a result, cells survive:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;High temperature&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;Loss of chaperones&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;Loss of major proteases&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;This model integrates classical SR, transcriptional regulation, and proteostasis into a single framework&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;Stringent-like enzymes in bacteria, often referred to as&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;RelA/SpoT Homologs (RSHs)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: windowtext&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent&quot;&gt;, are a family of proteins that regulate the &quot;stringent response&quot;. This response allows bacteria to adapt to stressful, nutrient-limiting conditions by shifting from a &quot;growth mode&quot; to a &quot;survival mode&quot; (slow growth)&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The rpoB mutation changes RNA polymerase stability and transcriptional behaviour in a way that becomes beneficial at high temperature.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This benefit emerges &lt;strong&gt;even though the mutation initially evolves for antibiotic resistance&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;HOW rpoB mutations help adaptation to heat (mechanisms)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. 🔧 Increased functional robustness of RNA polymerase at high temperature&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors show that the mutant RNAP:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Maintains &lt;strong&gt;better transcriptional activity at elevated temperatures&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Is &lt;strong&gt;less prone to heat-induced dysfunction&lt;/strong&gt; than wild-type RNAP
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why this matters at high temperature:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Heat destabilises protein–protein and protein–DNA interactions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
RNAP is a massive, multi-subunit complex → especially heat-sensitive
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A slightly altered β-subunit (RpoB) can &lt;strong&gt;shift RNAP conformational dynamics&lt;/strong&gt;, improving function when thermal motion increases
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Altered transcriptional fidelity and promoter usage&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper shows that the rpoB mutant:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Changes &lt;strong&gt;initiation efficiency&lt;/strong&gt; at certain promoters
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Alters &lt;strong&gt;elongation behaviour&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Modifies &lt;strong&gt;termination sensitivity&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At high temperature:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
DNA melting increases
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Promoter discrimination becomes noisier
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Transcription errors increase
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The rpoB mutation partially compensates for this&lt;/strong&gt;, producing:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
More stable transcription under heat stress
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Reduced mis-regulation of essential genes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;1. Isolation and identification of the rpoB58 mutant&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they did&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They selected &lt;strong&gt;rifampicin-resistant (Rifᴿ) mutants&lt;/strong&gt;, which commonly arise from mutations in &lt;strong&gt;rpoB&lt;/strong&gt;, the gene encoding the β-subunit of RNA polymerase.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutants were isolated under selective conditions (e.g. antibiotic exposure).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The rpoB allele was identified by &lt;strong&gt;DNA sequencing&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
rpoB mutations are known to alter RNAP behavior.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The authors wanted to find mutations that &lt;strong&gt;mimic the stringent response&lt;/strong&gt; without requiring (p)ppGpp.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they found&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They identified &lt;strong&gt;rpoB58&lt;/strong&gt;, a novel RNAP mutant.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This mutation caused RNAP to behave as if it were in a &lt;strong&gt;constitutive stringent-like state&lt;/strong&gt;, even without elevated (p)ppGpp.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;2. Growth assays under stress conditions (temperature sensitivity)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they did&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Compared growth of:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Wild type
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
dnaK or dnaJ mutants (lacking key chaperones)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
dnaK/J mutants carrying &lt;strong&gt;rpoB58&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cells were grown at:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Permissive temperature
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Non-permissive high temperatures (e.g. 42–47 °C)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Growth was measured by &lt;strong&gt;OD and colony formation&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Loss of DnaKJ causes protein misfolding, especially at high temperature.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
If rpoB58 improves protein homeostasis, it should &lt;strong&gt;rescue growth&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they found&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
dnaK/dnaJ mutants normally fail to grow at high temperature.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;rpoB58 strongly rescued growth&lt;/strong&gt;, even without major chaperones.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This indicated improved &lt;strong&gt;protein homeostasis capacity&lt;/strong&gt;, not improved folding machinery.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;3. Testing dependence on (p)ppGpp (RelA background)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they did&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Introduced rpoB58 into strains:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
With functional &lt;strong&gt;RelA&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
With defective &lt;strong&gt;relA&lt;/strong&gt; (unable to make (p)ppGpp)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Tested growth and stress tolerance.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
To determine whether rpoB58 requires (p)ppGpp to work.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they found&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
rpoB58 &lt;strong&gt;did not require (p)ppGpp&lt;/strong&gt; to restore protein homeostasis.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
RNAP with RpoB58 still responds to (p)ppGpp for classic stringent phenotypes, but &lt;strong&gt;protein homeostasis rescue was independent&lt;/strong&gt; of (p)ppGpp.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;4. Role of DksA (genetic dependency experiments)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they did&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Deleted &lt;strong&gt;dksA&lt;/strong&gt; in:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
dnaK/J mutants
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
dnaK/J + rpoB58 strains
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Measured growth at high temperature.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
DksA is a core stringent response factor that suppresses rRNA and ribosomal protein gene expression.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they found&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Without DksA, the ability of &lt;strong&gt;rpoB58 to rescue growth was dramatically reduced&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Overexpression of DksA improved growth of dnaK/J mutants.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Conclusion: &lt;strong&gt;DksA is essential for RNAP-mediated reduction in protein synthesis&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;5. Protease dependency (Lon and ClpP deletions)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they did&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Deleted major proteases:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Lon&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ClpP&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Tested whether rpoB58 still rescued growth.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
To see whether improved protein homeostasis came from &lt;strong&gt;increased protein degradation&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they found&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
rpoB58 still improved growth without Lon or ClpP.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Therefore, rescue was &lt;strong&gt;not due to enhanced proteolysis&lt;/strong&gt;, but reduced burden of new protein synthesis.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;6. RNA-seq analysis (global transcription profiling)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they did&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Performed &lt;strong&gt;RNA-seq&lt;/strong&gt; comparing:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Wild-type RNAP
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
RNAP with RpoB58
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Looked at rRNA, ribosomal protein genes, and stress genes.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
To confirm whether rpoB58 truly induces a &lt;strong&gt;stringent-like transcriptional program&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they found&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;rpoB58 caused:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Downregulation of rRNA and ribosomal protein genes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Activation of stress-mitigation pathways
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The transcriptional profile closely matched the &lt;strong&gt;stringent response&lt;/strong&gt;, even without (p)ppGpp.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;7. Chloramphenicol treatment (direct protein synthesis inhibition)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they did&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Treated dnaJ mutants with &lt;strong&gt;sub-lethal doses of chloramphenicol&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Measured growth at non-permissive temperature.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
To test whether &lt;strong&gt;simply slowing translation&lt;/strong&gt; is enough to rescue protein homeostasis.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they found&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Partial inhibition of protein synthesis &lt;strong&gt;rescued growth&lt;/strong&gt;, mimicking rpoB58.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Strong evidence that &lt;strong&gt;reduced protein synthesis rate is the key mechanism&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;8. Growth in minimal medium&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they did&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Grew cells in &lt;strong&gt;minimal medium&lt;/strong&gt; (slow growth, low translation capacity).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Compared growth of dnaK/J mutants with and without rpoB58.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Minimal medium naturally reduces ribosome production and protein synthesis.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;What they found&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Growth defects of dnaK/J mutants were &lt;strong&gt;reversed&lt;/strong&gt; in minimal medium.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Wild-type rpoB behaved like rpoB58 under slow-growth conditions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Reinforced the conclusion that &lt;strong&gt;lower protein synthesis rebalances proteostasis&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Big picture: what the methods collectively show&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Every experiment converges on one idea:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cells don’t need better folding machinery — they need to stop making so many proteins.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
rpoB58 reprograms RNAP into a stringent-like state
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This reduces ribosome production and translation load
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Protein homeostasis improves even without chaperones or proteases
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Thermotolerance increases as a consequence
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
