        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_135"/>
        <dc:title>The Reproducibility of Adaptation in the Light of Experimental Evolution with Whole Genome Sequencing</dc:title>
        <dc:date>2014</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>http://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-94-007-7347-9_11</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-12 00:57:04</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>Series Title: Advances in Experimental Medicine and Biology
DOI: 10.1007/978-94-007-7347-9_11</dc:description>
        <bib:pages>211-231</bib:pages>
    </bib:BookSection>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_137">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-citation-items=&quot;%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FVV2I7QS2%22%5D%2C%22itemData%22%3A%7B%22id%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FVV2I7QS2%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22chapter%22%2C%22container-title%22%3A%22Ecological%20Genomics%22%2C%22event-place%22%3A%22Dordrecht%22%2C%22ISBN%22%3A%22978-94-007-7346-2%22%2C%22language%22%3A%22en%22%2C%22note%22%3A%22collection-title%3A%20Advances%20in%20Experimental%20Medicine%20and%20Biology%5CnDOI%3A%2010.1007%2F978-94-007-7347-9_11%22%2C%22page%22%3A%22211-231%22%2C%22publisher%22%3A%22Springer%20Netherlands%22%2C%22publisher-place%22%3A%22Dordrecht%22%2C%22source%22%3A%22DOI.org%20(Crossref)%22%2C%22title%22%3A%22The%20Reproducibility%20of%20Adaptation%20in%20the%20Light%20of%20Experimental%20Evolution%20with%20Whole%20Genome%20Sequencing%22%2C%22URL%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Flink.springer.com%2F10.1007%2F978-94-007-7347-9_11%22%2C%22volume%22%3A%22781%22%2C%22editor%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Landry%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Christian%20R.%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Aubin-Horth%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Nadia%22%7D%5D%2C%22author%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Achaz%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Guillaume%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Rodriguez-Verdugo%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Alejandra%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Gaut%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Brandon%20S.%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Tenaillon%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Olivier%22%7D%5D%2C%22accessed%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222026%22%2C2%2C12%5D%5D%7D%2C%22issued%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222014%22%5D%5D%7D%7D%7D%5D&quot; data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;“as the population size gets bigger, more and more beneficial mutations will be simultaneously present in the population, and may compete in what is called ‘clonal interference’ (Gerrish and Lenski 1998; Desai and Fisher 2007). As the fittest mutations outcompete other mutations, both forms of convergence will be higher because mutations will be fixed approximately according to the ranks of their fitness effects. If populations get even larger, double mutants will appear, making path convergence difficult to observe. Thus, both state and path convergence are most likely to be observed &lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FW872PBMG%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%22217%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A6%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B59.541%2C657.1151158%2C255.12377442000025%2C665.8722412000001%5D%2C%5B59.541%2C644.6140453200001%2C255.1297519800004%2C653.3711707200001%5D%2C%5B59.541%2C632.1129748400001%2C182.64685568000024%2C640.8701002400002%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FVV2I7QS2%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%22217%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“in populations that are large but not so large as to commonly house double-mutants (Paixao et al. manuscript under preparation).”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FVV2I7QS2%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%22217%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Achaz et al., 2014, p. 217&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FW872PBMG%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%22217%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A6%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B440.4889062799989%2C644.61148754%2C462.0639128399988%2C653.36861294%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C632.11041706%2C462.0469764199999%2C640.8675424600001%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C619.6093465800001%2C462.05195771999894%2C628.3664719800001%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C607.1082761000001%2C462.0808492599995%2C615.8654015000002%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C594.6161719600001%2C462.05096145999977%2C603.3732973600002%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C582.1151014800001%2C462.0718829199999%2C590.8722268800002%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C569.6140310000002%2C462.07686421999995%2C578.3711564000002%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C557.1129605200002%2C462.0509614599999%2C565.8700859200003%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C544.6118900400003%2C462.0698903999987%2C553.3690154400003%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C532.1108195600003%2C462.06391283999903%2C540.8679449600004%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C519.6097490800004%2C462.06490909999866%2C528.3668744800004%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C507.1086786000004%2C462.03601755999875%2C515.8658040000004%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C494.6076081200004%2C462.05893153999966%2C503.36473352000036%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C482.1155039800004%2C462.0479726800001%2C490.8726293800004%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C469.6144335000004%2C462.05195771999865%2C478.37155890000037%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C457.1133630200004%2C462.08184551999983%2C465.87048842000036%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C444.6122925400004%2C462.0678978799987%2C453.36941794000035%5D%2C%5B266.46818704%2C432.1112220600004%2C376.1733494599993%2C440.86834746000034%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FVV2I7QS2%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%22217%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“when one examines the molecular details of adaptive events, it is often difficult to be sure that they resulted from the same selective pressures; that is, did the different organisms truly experience and adapt to the same environmental challenge? As a consequence, if different adaptive responses are observed among species or among populations, it is difficult to know if they result from slight differences in selective pressures or from the stochastic nature of adaptation. Alternatively, if similarities are observed, it is difficult to know if they result from the same selective pressure or appeared as collateral effects of different selective pressures. Therefore, to truly understand convergence, adaptive landscapes and the stochastic nature of the adaptive process, highly controlled environments are necessary”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FVV2I7QS2%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%22217%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Achaz et al., 2014, p. 217&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FW872PBMG%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%22223%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A12%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B101.41459670000005%2C594.6188707%2C255.1245633200003%2C603.3759961000001%5D%2C%5B59.54178889999997%2C582.11780022%2C255.1295446200002%2C590.8749256200001%5D%2C%5B59.54278515999998%2C569.6167297400001%2C255.1056343800004%2C578.3738551400002%5D%2C%5B59.54278515999998%2C557.1156592600001%2C255.12755210000034%2C565.8727846600002%5D%2C%5B59.54278515999998%2C544.6145887800002%2C255.1395072200004%2C553.3717141800003%5D%2C%5B59.54278515999998%2C532.1135183000002%2C255.1265558400003%2C540.8706437000003%5D%2C%5B59.54278515999998%2C519.6124478200003%2C255.1116119400004%2C528.3695732200003%5D%2C%5B59.54278515999998%2C507.11137734000033%2C255.13253340000017%2C515.8685027400003%5D%2C%5B59.54278515999998%2C494.6103068600003%2C255.1126082000003%2C503.36743226000027%5D%2C%5B59.54278515999998%2C482.11820272000034%2C255.1255595800004%2C490.8753281200003%5D%2C%5B59.54278515999998%2C469.61713224000033%2C255.13153714000032%2C478.3742576400003%5D%2C%5B59.54278515999998%2C457.1160617600003%2C230.54981790000033%2C465.87318716000027%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FVV2I7QS2%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%22223%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“Interestingly a systemic analysis of the impact of a mutation in rho on the fitness effect of transposon-based gene inactivation (Freddolino et al. 2012) revealed that a single mutation in that gene was enough to drastically change the pattern of selection over the whole genome. This confirms the idea that affecting global regulators such as rho, which is a termination factor, or RNA polymerase can have a large epistatic impact by making some previously beneficial mutations deleterious and by, reciprocally, making some previously deleterious mutations beneficial”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FVV2I7QS2%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%22223%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Achaz et al., 2014, p. 223&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FW872PBMG%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%22228%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A17%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B142.31326958000008%2C282.20939378000014%2C255.12975198000015%2C290.9665191800001%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C269.70832330000013%2C255.14170710000045%2C278.4654487000001%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C257.2072528200001%2C255.1257669400004%2C265.96437822000007%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C244.70618234000008%2C255.1187931200004%2C253.4633077400001%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C232.20511186000007%2C255.11181930000012%2C240.96223726000008%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C219.70404138000006%2C255.11580434000047%2C228.46116678000007%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C207.20297090000005%2C255.10285296000046%2C215.96009630000006%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C194.70190042000004%2C255.11082304000038%2C203.45902582000005%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C182.20082994000003%2C255.12576694000003%2C190.95795534000004%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C169.70872580000002%2C255.13473328000035%2C178.46585120000003%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C157.20765532000001%2C255.1277594600004%2C165.96478072000002%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C144.70658484%2C255.12676320000045%2C153.46371024%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C132.20551436%2C255.12477068000035%2C140.96263976%5D%2C%5B59.541000000000054%2C119.70444388%2C111.60654386000007%2C128.46156928%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FVV2I7QS2%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%22228%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“Reynolds (2000) studied the phenotypic effect of compensatory mutations in the RNA polymerase following the acquisition of a mutation providing rifampicin resistance. She convincingly showed that different mutants improved the efficiency of transcription that had been deteriorated by the resistance mutation. This kind of experiment is interesting but can only be applied to genes that have been identified as a target of adaptation. However, as only a specific phenotype is studied, this approach may hinder the understanding of collateral phenotypic effects and therefore provides only a partial vision of convergence.”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FVV2I7QS2%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%22228%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Achaz et al., 2014, p. 228&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;1. Central Question&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper addresses a major evolutionary question:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If we evolve multiple populations under identical conditions, how reproducible is adaptation at the genomic level?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With whole-genome sequencing (WGS), researchers can now track &lt;strong&gt;every mutation&lt;/strong&gt; that arises during experimental evolution. The authors review and synthesize results from such studies to understand:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
How predictable adaptation is
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
At what biological levels repeatability occurs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
What determines evolutionary convergence
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;2. Background: Experimental Evolution + WGS&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Experimental evolution typically involves:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Starting with identical populations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Evolving them in the same controlled environment
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Sequencing genomes after adaptation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This allows researchers to compare:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Which mutations appear
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Whether the same genes are repeatedly targeted
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Whether the same exact mutations arise
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is essentially a real-world version of “replaying the tape of life.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;3. Key Concept 1: Levels of Reproducibility&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One of the most important ideas in the paper is that &lt;strong&gt;reproducibility depends on the biological level examined&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors distinguish between:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. Nucleotide-level convergence&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Same exact base change appears independently.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;→ Rare, but does occur (especially when mutation supply is limited).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. Gene-level convergence&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Different mutations in the same gene across populations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;→ Very common.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. Pathway-level convergence&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Different genes in the same functional pathway are targeted.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;→ Also common.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Main Conclusion&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reproducibility increases as you move up in biological scale:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Exact mutation &amp;lt; Gene &amp;lt; Pathway &amp;lt; Phenotype&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So evolution is often &lt;strong&gt;functionally reproducible&lt;/strong&gt;, even if genetically variable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;4. Key Concept 2: Drivers of Parallel Evolution&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper identifies factors that increase reproducibility:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;A. Strong Selection&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If only a few mutations give a large fitness benefit:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Those mutations repeatedly arise
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Parallel evolution is high
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Example: antibiotic resistance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;B. Limited Mutational Target Size&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If only certain genes can improve fitness:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Evolution is constrained
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Convergence is likely
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;C. Large Population Size&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Large populations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Explore more mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Increase probability of repeated beneficial mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Promote parallelism
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;D. Clonal Interference&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In large asexual populations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Multiple beneficial mutations compete
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Different lineages may fix different mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Reduces exact repeatability
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;5. Key Concept 3: The Role of Epistasis&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Epistasis = interaction between mutations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors emphasize:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Sign epistasis can restrict accessible evolutionary paths
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Some mutations only beneficial after prior mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Historical contingency shapes trajectories
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Even with epistasis, convergence at gene or pathway level remains common.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;6. Whole-Genome Sequencing Insights&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;WGS revealed several important patterns:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. Soft Sweeps&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Multiple beneficial mutations in the same gene may arise simultaneously.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. Genetic Heterogeneity&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Different populations often use different genetic solutions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. Hidden Parallelism&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Even when different genes mutate, they may affect the same biological function.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Without WGS, many of these patterns would be invisible.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;7. Determinism vs Contingency&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper frames the debate as:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Determinism&lt;/strong&gt; → Same outcomes repeatedly
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Contingency&lt;/strong&gt; → Historical accidents matter
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Their conclusion is nuanced:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Short-term adaptation in stable environments is often surprisingly reproducible — especially at higher biological levels.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Exact mutations are often unpredictable
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Long-term evolution may diverge more
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thus evolution is:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Deterministic in function, stochastic in detail.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;8. Broader Implications&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors suggest:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Adaptation is constrained by genetic architecture
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Functional targets of selection are limited
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Evolutionary paths are biased by mutation rate and genome structure
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This has implications for:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Antibiotic resistance prediction
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Viral evolution
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Cancer evolution
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Adaptive forecasting
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;9. Overall Take-Home Message&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Whole-genome sequencing shows that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Evolution is more reproducible than expected.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Convergence is common at gene and pathway levels.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Exact mutations are less predictable.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Population genetic parameters strongly shape repeatability.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If we replayed evolution in the lab:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We likely wouldn’t see identical genomes.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
But we would often see similar biological solutions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
