        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_206"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_207"/>
        <dc:title>An Additional L451G452N453 in the RpoB Protein Suppressed the Synthetic Lethality in &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt; at 37 Degrees Caused by Depletion of DnaK/J and Trigger Factor</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Escherichia coli depletion of chaperone trigger factor and DnaK/J were not viable at 37°C, but viable below 30°C. Among the engineered E. coli depleted of trigger factor and DnaK/J, one strain Z625, exhibited survival at 37°C, while another strain Z629 only survived below 30°C. Comparative analysis of fatty acid profiles of Z625 and Z629 revealed absence of numerous saturated fatty acids in Z625 as compared to the wild‐type E. coli BW25113. In addition, increased unsaturated fatty acids were present in Z625, whereas the fatty acids profile of Z629 closely resembled that of BW25113. Whole genome sequencing revealed a 9‐bp insertion in rpoB of Z625. Combined structural analysis of simulated RpoB protein bearing the amino acid sequence L451G452N453 insertion and susceptibility analysis to rifampicin suggested that the insertion did not disturb the individual RpoB structure as beta subunit of RNA polymerase. Comparative transcriptomic analysis of Z625 and Z629 suggested that this insertion impacted transcription of the overall RNA polymerase in Z625, leading to potential repression of some genes whose overexpression was toxic to E. coli. Additionally, Z625 exhibited distinctive metabolic adaptations, likely contributing to its survival at 37°C. In summary, our study elucidated one LGN insertion in rpoB that impacts transcriptional regulation in E. coli, thereby explaining the survival of E. coli depletion of trigger factor and DnaK/J at 37°C, and these founding suggested that some simple mutations in critical genes like rpoB might play an important role in driving adaptive evolution.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>11/2024</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jobm.202400253</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-01-22 01:23:44</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>e2400253</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_206">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Notably, when E. coli was subjected to thermal&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;stress and evolved at 42°C across 114 separate populations, all&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;adaptive mutations were exclusively located within the rpoB gene&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Core question&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Why can a rare &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; strain lacking &lt;strong&gt;three major chaperones&lt;/strong&gt; (Trigger Factor + DnaK/J) survive at &lt;strong&gt;37 °C&lt;/strong&gt;, when such strains are normally lethal?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Central finding&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;9-bp insertion in rpoB&lt;/strong&gt;, encoding an extra &lt;strong&gt;L451–G452–N453 (LGN)&lt;/strong&gt; tripeptide in the β subunit of RNA polymerase, &lt;strong&gt;suppresses synthetic lethality&lt;/strong&gt; by globally rewiring transcription, metabolism, and membrane composition—without disrupting RNA polymerase structure or rifampicin sensitivity.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Biological background&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Chaperone essentiality&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Trigger Factor (TF)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;DnaK/J&lt;/strong&gt; are core components of the &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; protein-folding network.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Deleting &lt;strong&gt;either&lt;/strong&gt; TF &lt;strong&gt;or&lt;/strong&gt; DnaK/J → cells survive at 37 °C.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Deleting &lt;strong&gt;both&lt;/strong&gt; → massive protein aggregation → &lt;strong&gt;lethal at 37 °C&lt;/strong&gt;, viable only ≤30 °C.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;RNA polymerase as an evolutionary “lever”&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;RpoB&lt;/strong&gt;, the β subunit of RNA polymerase, controls transcription genome-wide.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutations in rpoB are:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Common in evolution experiments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Often pleiotropic
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Known to compensate for unrelated cellular defects
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Experimental setup&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key strains&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;BW25113&lt;/strong&gt; – wild type
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Z629&lt;/strong&gt; – Δtig ΔdnaK/J, viable only ≤30 °C
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Z625&lt;/strong&gt; – Δtig ΔdnaK/J, &lt;em&gt;unexpectedly viable at 37 °C&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Major approaches&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Whole-genome sequencing (PacBio + DNBSEQ)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Transcriptomics (microarrays)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fatty acid methyl ester (FAME) profiling
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Rifampicin susceptibility testing
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Structural modeling of RpoB
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Discovery of the suppressor mutation&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Genome sequencing&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Z625 differs from Z629 by a &lt;strong&gt;single notable change&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;9-bp insertion in rpoB&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Encodes &lt;strong&gt;LGN (L451–G452–N453)&lt;/strong&gt; insertion in RpoB
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Structural consequences&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Protein modeling shows:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Minimal structural deviation&lt;/strong&gt; (RMSD ≈ 0.05)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Insertion lies &lt;strong&gt;outside rifampicin-binding regions&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rifampicin MIC unchanged&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;→ RNAP structure and drug binding intact
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key implication:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;The mutation alters &lt;em&gt;function&lt;/em&gt;, not &lt;em&gt;structure&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Phenotypic consequences of the rpoB insertion&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. Suppression of synthetic lethality&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Z625 survives at 37 °C despite lacking TF + DnaK/J
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Z629 (no rpoB insertion) cannot
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. Massive transcriptional rewiring&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors perform &lt;strong&gt;four transcriptomic comparisons&lt;/strong&gt; to separate effects of:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Chaperone deletion
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Temperature
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
rpoB insertion
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They classify genes into &lt;strong&gt;four classes&lt;/strong&gt;, revealing that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;a. Chaperone deletion induces compensation&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Upregulation of:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
groEL/ES
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ibpA/B
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
clpB, hslUV
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Seen in &lt;em&gt;both&lt;/em&gt; Z625 and Z629
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Represents a general proteostasis stress response
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;b. rpoB insertion causes global transcriptional shifts&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Hundreds of genes affected &lt;strong&gt;only in Z625&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Strong effects on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Ribosomal proteins
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Transcription machinery
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Respiration
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fatty acid metabolism
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Crucially:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Many genes are &lt;strong&gt;downregulated&lt;/strong&gt;, especially those encoding:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Membrane proteins
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Redox enzymes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Transporters
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Interpretation: the rpoB insertion &lt;strong&gt;represses expression of proteins whose overproduction would be toxic without chaperones&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. Metabolic reprogramming&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Central carbon metabolism&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Glycolysis ↓&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;TCA cycle ↑&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Aerobic respiration ↑&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Anaerobic respiration ↓&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This shifts metabolism toward:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Higher ATP yield
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
More efficient catabolism of amino acids and fatty acids released from misfolded proteins
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;4. Dramatic membrane remodeling&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Fatty acid profiling (key result)&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Z625 shows:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Strong increase in unsaturated fatty acids (UFAs)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Loss of cyclopropane and saturated fatty acids&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Z629’s lipid profile ≈ wild type
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Transcriptional basis&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Upregulation of:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
accA (malonyl-CoA production)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
fadA/B/E/J (fatty acid degradation)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
No major change in canonical UFA regulators (fabA/B, fadR)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Functional outcome&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
More UFAs → &lt;strong&gt;higher membrane fluidity&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Better tolerance of heat stress at 37 °C
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Integrated survival model&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors propose that the &lt;strong&gt;LGN insertion in rpoB enables survival via three coupled mechanisms&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. Proteostasis relief&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Suppresses transcription of aggregation-prone proteins
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Enhances chaperone/protease compensation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. Metabolic optimization&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Shifts energy metabolism toward aerobic respiration
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Efficient degradation of misfolded proteins and excess fatty acids
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. Membrane adaptation&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Increased UFAs stabilize membranes at high temperature
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Together, these changes &lt;strong&gt;compensate for the loss of three major chaperones&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Conceptual significance&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why this paper matters&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Demonstrates suppressor evolution&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A &lt;em&gt;single&lt;/em&gt;, simple insertion rescues a lethal genotype
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Highlights rpoB as a global compensator&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Essential genes can buffer unrelated cellular failures
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Links transcriptional repression to survival&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Not all adaptation is upregulation—&lt;em&gt;strategic downregulation matters&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Connects proteostasis, metabolism, and membrane biology&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Survival emerges from systems-level rewiring
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Relation to earlier work&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This study directly echoes:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Rodríguez-Verdugo et al. (2016): early rpoB mutations restore gene expression under thermal stress
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ppGpp-mimicking rpoB mutations in stringent response mutants
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Common theme:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;rpoB mutations are restorative, not innovative—they rebalance the system.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;One-sentence takeaway&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A small insertion in RNA polymerase β rewires transcription, metabolism, and membrane composition to suppress otherwise lethal chaperone loss in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;, revealing how mutations in global regulators can rescue catastrophic cellular defects.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
