        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_213"/>
        <dc:title>First-Step Mutations during Adaptation Restore the Expression of Hundreds of Genes</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>The temporal change of phenotypes during the adaptive process remains largely unexplored, as do the genetic changes that affect these phenotypic changes. Here we focused on three mutations that rose to high frequency in the early stages of adaptation within 12 Escherichia coli populations subjected to thermal stress (42 C). All the mutations were in the rpoB gene, which encodes the RNA polymerase beta subunit. For each mutation, we measured the growth curves and gene expression (mRNAseq) of clones at 42 C. We also compared growth and gene expression with their ancestor under unstressed (37 C) and stressed conditions (42 C). Each of the three mutations changed the expression of hundreds of genes and conferred large ﬁtness advantages, apparently through the restoration of global gene expression from the stressed toward the prestressed state. These three mutations had a similar effect on gene expression as another single mutation in a distinct domain of the rpoB protein. Finally, we compared the phenotypic characteristics of one mutant, I572L, with two high-temperature adapted clones that have this mutation plus additional background mutations. The background mutations increased ﬁtness, but they did not substantially change gene expression. We conclude that early mutations in a global transcriptional regulator cause extensive changes in gene expression, many of which are likely under positive selection for their effect in restoring the prestress physiology.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>01/2016</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://academic.oup.com/mbe/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/molbev/msv228</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-01-22 03:13:50</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>25-39</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_212">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Nevertheless, two previous studies have explored the phys- iological acclimation of E. coli to high temperature and re- ported upregulation of heat shock genes (Gunasekera et al. 2008; Sandberg et al. 2014). The discrepancy among studies may be explained by differences in genetic backgrounds (E. coli K-12 strain vs. E. coli B strain), by differences in temper- ature (42 C vs. 43 C in Gunasekera et al. 2008), by differences in culture conditions (ours favor microaerophiles), and by differences in sampling. For example, in our study bacterial clones were allowed to acclimate at 42 C for 1 day before being sampled the next day during midexponential phase at 42 C. Thus, the bacteria spent one complete cycle of growth (lag, exponential, and stationary phase) at 42 C, in addition to the approximately half cycle of growth (lag and half expo- nential phase) at 42 C (in total ~1.5 days). For other studies, the time that the bacteria spent at high temperature before the RNA extraction is unclear (Sandberg et al. 2014). Similarly, we have carefully documented the growth curve of the an- cestor at 42 C to help ensure that our RNAseq samples orig- inated from the exponential growth phase (supplementary fig. S8, Supplementary Material online). Other studies report to have sampled during midexponential phase but have not reported growth curves. Under normal conditions growth curves may not be necessary, but the exponential phase can be very short under extreme stress. It is thus possible that previous studies have reported GE on slightly different phases of the growth cycle&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In conclusion, the ancestor acclimates to thermal stress by launching alternative GE programs (Lopez-Maury et al. 2008). One involves the downregulation of growth-related path- ways, potentially resulting in energy conservation. The other involves the upregulation of stress genes that are involved in repair and metabolic adjustments to high temperature.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Big-picture idea&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; is pushed into a stressful environment (42 °C), its physiology goes haywire: growth slows and thousands of genes change expression. This paper shows that &lt;strong&gt;the very first beneficial mutations&lt;/strong&gt;—often single-point mutations in a global transcriptional regulator—can &lt;strong&gt;dramatically restore gene expression toward the pre-stress state&lt;/strong&gt;, and that this restoration explains much of their large fitness benefit.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In short:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Early adaptation isn’t about inventing new biology—it’s about fixing what stress broke.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Experimental context&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This study builds on a massive evolution experiment:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
114 replicate &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; populations evolved at &lt;strong&gt;42 °C&lt;/strong&gt; for &lt;strong&gt;2,000 generations&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Many populations acquired mutations in &lt;strong&gt;rpoB&lt;/strong&gt;, which encodes the &lt;strong&gt;β subunit of RNA polymerase (RNAP)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Certain &lt;strong&gt;rpoB mutations appeared very early&lt;/strong&gt;, swept rapidly, and conferred ~20% fitness advantages
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors zoom in on these &lt;strong&gt;“first-step” mutations&lt;/strong&gt; and ask:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
How do they affect growth?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
How do they affect genome-wide gene expression?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
How do their effects compare to later mutations?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Conceptual framework: four stages&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors define four physiological/genetic states:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;WT (prestress)&lt;/strong&gt; – ancestor at 37 °C
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AC (acclimated)&lt;/strong&gt; – ancestor grown at 42 °C (stress response, no evolution)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;1ST (first-step)&lt;/strong&gt; – single rpoB mutant at 42 °C
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;EVO (evolved)&lt;/strong&gt; – clones after 2,000 generations at 42 °C
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This lets them track &lt;strong&gt;phenotypic change over time&lt;/strong&gt;, not just endpoints.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;What happens during heat acclimation (no evolution yet)?&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When the ancestor is shifted from 37 °C → 42 °C:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Growth collapses&lt;/strong&gt;: longer lag phase, slower growth, lower yield
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Massive transcriptional disruption&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
~1,700 genes differentially expressed
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Downregulation of:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Ribosomal proteins
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Translation machinery
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Amino acid biosynthesis
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Motility genes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Upregulation of:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Stress-response genes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Catabolic pathways
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Surprisingly, &lt;strong&gt;classic heat-shock genes are mostly not upregulated&lt;/strong&gt; during long-term acclimation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Interpretation:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;The cells behave like they’re stuck in a low-growth, quasi–stationary phase. This is the &lt;em&gt;phenotype selection acts on&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;First-step mutations: what genes and why they matter&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors focus on three parallel mutations at &lt;strong&gt;codon 572 of rpoB&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
I572F
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
I572L
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
I572N
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Appear early
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Sweep rapidly
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Confer large fitness benefits at 42 °C
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They also examine &lt;strong&gt;I966S&lt;/strong&gt;, a mutation in a different domain of RNAP.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Growth effects of first-step mutations&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Compared to the ancestor at 42 °C, first-step mutants:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Grow &lt;strong&gt;significantly faster&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Reach higher yields
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Still perform worse than the ancestor at 37 °C (stress isn’t fully “solved”)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So these mutations deliver a &lt;strong&gt;big chunk of the total adaptive benefit&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;The core result: massive gene expression restoration&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each &lt;em&gt;single&lt;/em&gt; rpoB mutation:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Alters expression of &lt;strong&gt;hundreds to thousands of genes&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
These changes are &lt;strong&gt;highly pleiotropic&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Most importantly:&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gene expression shifts back toward the 37 °C (prestress) state&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;How they show this&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Principal Component Analysis&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Mutants cluster closer to the 37 °C ancestor than to the stressed ancestor.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Regression analysis&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
X-axis: change caused by heat stress (37 → 42 °C)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Y-axis: change caused by the mutation (mutant vs stressed ancestor)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Slopes ≈ &lt;strong&gt;–0.6 to –0.7&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Near –1 = perfect restoration
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
0 = no relationship
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is strong evidence that mutations &lt;strong&gt;undo&lt;/strong&gt; stress-induced expression changes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Categories of gene expression change&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each gene falls into one of four classes:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Restored&lt;/strong&gt; – expression pushed back toward prestress levels
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reinforced&lt;/strong&gt; – stress response exaggerated
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unrestored&lt;/strong&gt; – stress response unchanged
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Novel&lt;/strong&gt; – new expression change not seen during acclimation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Key findings:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Restoration dominates&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Novel expression is rare&lt;/strong&gt; (~2–3%)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Reinforcement is almost nonexistent
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So adaptation here is &lt;strong&gt;not innovative&lt;/strong&gt;, it’s &lt;strong&gt;corrective&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Convergence across different RNAP mutations&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Different amino acid substitutions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Different RNAP domains (active site vs flap domain)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All four rpoB mutations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Restore largely overlapping sets of genes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Share ~⅓ of restored genes across all mutants
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Share ~86% with at least one other mutant
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This shows &lt;strong&gt;strong phenotypic convergence&lt;/strong&gt;, even when mutations differ structurally.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;What about later mutations?&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors compare:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Single first-step mutant (I572L)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fully evolved clones&lt;/strong&gt; that contain I572L + many other mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Results:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Later mutations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Further improve growth
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Barely change gene expression&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Only ~10–20 genes differ between first-step and evolved clones
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Global expression patterns are almost identical
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The first-step mutation does nearly all the transcriptional “rewiring.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Interpretation and significance&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Main conclusions&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;First-step adaptive mutations can have enormous phenotypic effects&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
These effects are largely &lt;strong&gt;restorative&lt;/strong&gt;, not novel
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Global regulators (like RNAP) are prime early targets of selection
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Later adaptation fine-tunes fitness without large transcriptional shifts
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Why this paper matters&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Challenges the idea that adaptation is mainly about innovation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Shows that &lt;strong&gt;fixing stress-induced dysregulation&lt;/strong&gt; can yield large fitness gains
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Explains why mutations in transcriptional machinery are so common early in evolution experiments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Provides a rare &lt;strong&gt;time-resolved view of phenotypic evolution&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;One-sentence takeaway&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Early adaptation to stress in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; is driven by single, highly pleiotropic mutations in RNA polymerase that restore global gene expression toward a prestress state, delivering most of the fitness gains long before evolution “finishes.”&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
