    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_215">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;1. Central Question and Motivation&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper asks &lt;strong&gt;how long-term adaptation to high temperature affects antibiotic resistance in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. This question is motivated by two converging global pressures:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rising environmental temperatures&lt;/strong&gt; due to climate change
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Widespread antibiotic use&lt;/strong&gt; and increasing resistance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Because temperature stress and antibiotics can disrupt overlapping cellular processes (e.g., protein folding, transcription, translation), the authors hypothesize that &lt;strong&gt;evolution under thermal stress may indirectly reshape antibiotic resistance profiles&lt;/strong&gt;, even without direct antibiotic exposure 2024.02.27.582334v1.full.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;2. Experimental System and Approach&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Bacterial strains&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Ancestor: &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; B strain REL1206
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Experimental lines: &lt;strong&gt;114 independently evolved populations&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Evolution conditions: &lt;strong&gt;42.2°C for ~2,000 generations&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Origin: previously evolved lines from Tenaillon et al. (2012)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key measurements&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thermal performance curves (TPCs)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Growth measured across &lt;strong&gt;12–50°C&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Fitted using a reparameterized Brière model
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Extracted:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Optimal growth temperature (Topt)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Overall growth&lt;/strong&gt; (area under the TPC)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Antibiotic resistance&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Measured as &lt;strong&gt;IC50&lt;/strong&gt; (concentration inhibiting 50% growth)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;12 antibiotics&lt;/strong&gt;, spanning major drug classes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;IC50 assessed at:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;37°C&lt;/strong&gt; (non-stress, ancestral-like condition)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;42°C&lt;/strong&gt; (evolved, heat-stress condition)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This design allows the authors to test &lt;strong&gt;both evolutionary effects (heat adaptation)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;environmental effects (assay temperature)&lt;/strong&gt; on antibiotic resistance 2024.02.27.582334v1.full.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;3. Effects of Thermal Evolution on Growth&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;(a) Optimal growth temperature unexpectedly decreased&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mean Topt of heat-evolved strains: &lt;strong&gt;~36.2°C&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Ancestor Topt: &lt;strong&gt;~37.9°C&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;~79% of strains evolved a &lt;em&gt;lower&lt;/em&gt; optimal temperature&lt;/strong&gt;, contrary to expectations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Only ~21% showed increased Topt
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This contradicts the intuitive prediction that evolution at high temperature should shift optima upward.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;(b) Overall growth declined dramatically&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;~96% of heat-evolved strains showed reduced total growth&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;across the full temperature range
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Indicates &lt;strong&gt;fitness costs&lt;/strong&gt; associated with long-term heat adaptation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;(c) Trade-off between Topt and overall growth&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Significant &lt;strong&gt;negative correlation&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Strains with higher Topt tended to have &lt;em&gt;lower&lt;/em&gt; overall growth
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Suggests &lt;strong&gt;evolutionary trade-offs&lt;/strong&gt; rather than uniform thermal improvement
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Interpretation:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Heat adaptation appears to favor mechanisms that help cells &lt;em&gt;survive&lt;/em&gt; stress rather than maximize growth efficiency, possibly reflecting compensatory or plastic responses aimed at restoring “unstressed” physiology 2024.02.27.582334v1.full.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;4. Effects of Thermal Evolution on Antibiotic Resistance&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;(a) Heat evolution altered resistance profiles broadly&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;10 of 12 antibiotics&lt;/strong&gt; showed significant IC50 changes at 37°C
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Resistance changes included both:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Increased resistance&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., levofloxacin, gentamicin)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Increased sensitivity&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., ampicillin, tetracycline, trimethoprim)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thus, &lt;strong&gt;heat adaptation does not uniformly increase resistance&lt;/strong&gt;—it reshapes it in complex, drug-specific ways.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;(b) Temperature during testing matters&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For several antibiotics, IC50 differed significantly between &lt;strong&gt;37°C and 42°C&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Examples:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Levofloxacin and cefoxitin&lt;/strong&gt;: higher resistance at 42°C
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Clindamycin&lt;/strong&gt;: higher resistance at 37°C
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This demonstrates &lt;strong&gt;environment-dependent antibiotic efficacy&lt;/strong&gt;, even in the same genotype.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;5. Relationships Between Growth Traits and Antibiotic Resistance&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;(a) Mostly weak or absent correlations&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Changes in:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Optimal growth temperature
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Overall growth
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Antibiotic resistance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Were &lt;strong&gt;largely independent&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;(b) A few notable exceptions&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Positive correlation:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tobramycin resistance (42°C)&lt;/strong&gt; vs. increasing Topt
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Negative correlation:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nitrofurantoin resistance (37°C)&lt;/strong&gt; vs. overall growth
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, these were rare and antibiotic-specific.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key conclusion:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;There is &lt;strong&gt;no general rule&lt;/strong&gt; linking thermal adaptation, growth optimization, and antibiotic resistance—contrary to expectations from shared stress-response pathways 2024.02.27.582334v1.full.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;6. Conceptual and Evolutionary Implications&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why didn’t Topt increase?&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors propose several explanations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Compensatory evolution&lt;/strong&gt;: restoring cellular function toward the ancestral 37°C state
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Phenotypic plasticity&lt;/strong&gt;: allowing survival at high temperature without shifting optima
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mutation accumulation&lt;/strong&gt;: adaptive mutations for heat tolerance may reduce overall fitness
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Why are resistance effects so heterogeneous?&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Antibiotics differ in:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mechanisms of action
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Overlap with heat-stress pathways
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Heat adaptation may alter:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Protein folding
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Membrane properties
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Transcriptional regulation&lt;br&gt;in ways that affect drugs differently
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;7. Broader Significance&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The study shows that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Climate-relevant stressors can reshape antibiotic resistance&lt;/strong&gt;, even without antibiotics
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Resistance outcomes depend on both evolutionary history and current environment&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Predicting resistance evolution under climate change will require:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Multi-stressor frameworks
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Trait-based rather than single-metric approaches
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This work highlights the &lt;strong&gt;complex, non-intuitive nature of evolutionary responses to combined stressors&lt;/strong&gt;, with direct relevance to public health and microbial ecology&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
