    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_218">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-citation-items=&quot;%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FCUBRPFFG%22%5D%2C%22itemData%22%3A%7B%22id%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FCUBRPFFG%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22article-journal%22%2C%22abstract%22%3A%22Environmental%20factors%20like%20temperature%2C%20pressure%2C%20and%20pH%20partly%20shaped%20the%20evolution%20of%20life.%20As%20life%20progressed%2C%20new%20stressors%20(e.g.%2C%20poisons%20and%20antibiotics)%20arose%20as%20part%20of%20an%20arms%20race%20among%20organisms.%20Here%20we%20ask%20if%20cells%20co-opted%20existing%20mechanisms%20to%20respond%20to%20new%20stressors%2C%20or%20whether%20new%20responses%20evolved%20de%20novo.%20We%20use%20a%20network-clustering%20approach%20based%20purely%20on%20phenotypic%20growth%20measurements%20and%20interactions%20among%20the%20effects%20of%20stressors%20on%20population%20growth.%20We%20apply%20this%20method%20to%20two%20types%20of%20stressors%E2%80%94temperature%20and%20antibiotics%E2%80%94to%20discover%20the%20extent%20to%20which%20their%20cellular%20responses%20overlap%20in%20Escherichia%20coli.%20Our%20clustering%20reveals%20that%20responses%20to%20low%20and%20high%20temperatures%20are%20clearly%20separated%2C%20and%20each%20is%20grouped%20with%20responses%20to%20antibiotics%20that%20have%20similar%20effects%20to%20cold%20or%20heat%2C%20respectively.%20As%20further%20support%2C%20we%20use%20a%20library%20of%20transcriptional%20%EF%AC%82uorescent%20reporters%20to%20con%EF%AC%81rm%20heat-shock%20and%20cold-shock%20genes%20are%20induced%20by%20antibiotics.%20We%20also%20show%20strains%20evolved%20at%20high%20temperatures%20are%20more%20sensitive%20to%20antibiotics%20that%20mimic%20the%20effects%20of%20cold.%20Taken%20together%2C%20our%20results%20strongly%20suggest%20that%20temperature%20stress%20responses%20have%20been%20co-opted%20to%20deal%20with%20antibiotic%20stress.%22%2C%22container-title%22%3A%22The%20ISME%20Journal%22%2C%22DOI%22%3A%2210.1038%2Fs41396-018-0241-7%22%2C%22ISSN%22%3A%221751-7362%2C%201751-7370%22%2C%22issue%22%3A%221%22%2C%22language%22%3A%22en%22%2C%22license%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Facademic.oup.com%2Fpages%2Fstandard-publication-reuse-rights%22%2C%22page%22%3A%2212-23%22%2C%22source%22%3A%22DOI.org%20(Crossref)%22%2C%22title%22%3A%22Stressor%20interaction%20networks%20suggest%20antibiotic%20resistance%20co-opted%20from%20stress%20responses%20to%20temperature%22%2C%22URL%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Facademic.oup.com%2Fismej%2Farticle%2F13%2F1%2F12-23%2F7475053%22%2C%22volume%22%3A%2213%22%2C%22author%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Cruz-Loya%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Mauricio%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Kang%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Tina%20Manzhu%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Lozano%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Natalie%20Ann%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Watanabe%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Rina%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Tekin%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Elif%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Damoiseaux%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Robert%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Savage%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Van%20M%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Yeh%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Pamela%20J%22%7D%5D%2C%22accessed%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222026%22%2C1%2C22%5D%5D%7D%2C%22issued%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222019%22%2C1%2C1%5D%5D%7D%7D%7D%5D&quot; data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F3TDSSF3T%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%2213%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A1%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B186.06464858244587%2C672.7583364368179%2C289.09785507340956%2C681.5254242168179%5D%2C%5B51.02260616665896%2C660.2861583079228%2C289.11977378911945%2C669.0532460879228%5D%2C%5B51.02260616665896%2C647.8139801790278%2C289.05202842244967%2C656.5810679590278%5D%2C%5B51.02260616665896%2C635.3985887690818%2C289.04903940322293%2C644.1656765490818%5D%2C%5B51.02260616665896%2C622.9264106401868%2C289.03110701607534%2C631.6934984201868%5D%2C%5B51.02260616665896%2C610.4542325112917%2C289.09686035863376%2C619.2213202912917%5D%2C%5B51.02260616665896%2C597.7200379093457%2C145.25186344096292%2C607.6826376593458%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FCUBRPFFG%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%2213%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“In addition to functional overlap, there are compelling reasons for cells to evolve a relatively small suite of stress responses to multiple types of stressors. Developing a novel stress response requires investment in terms of genetic material (i.e., information), protein production, time to evolve, and energy to support simultaneous responses”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FCUBRPFFG%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%2213%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Cruz-Loya et al., 2019, p. 13&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Do bacteria evolve &lt;strong&gt;antibiotic resistance by re-using (co-opting) ancient stress-response pathways&lt;/strong&gt;, particularly temperature stress responses, rather than evolving entirely new mechanisms?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors test this idea in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; by integrating:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Phenotypic growth interactions&lt;/strong&gt; between antibiotics and temperature
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Network clustering&lt;/strong&gt; of stressor interactions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Gene expression profiling&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Antibiotic resistance phenotypes of heat-adapted strains&lt;/strong&gt;, including strains with known &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Main conceptual framework&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper argues that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Temperature stress is ancient&lt;/strong&gt;, whereas antibiotics are evolutionarily recent.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Cellular systems that evolved to handle &lt;strong&gt;heat or cold stress&lt;/strong&gt; may also buffer or resist antibiotics that damage similar cellular targets.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Antibiotic resistance can therefore emerge &lt;strong&gt;indirectly&lt;/strong&gt;, as a by-product of adaptation to environmental stress.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Key findings (overall)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. Temperature and antibiotics cluster by &lt;em&gt;physiological effect&lt;/em&gt;, not chemical class&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using &lt;strong&gt;stressor interaction networks (SINs)&lt;/strong&gt;, the authors show that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Low temperatures&lt;/strong&gt; cluster with:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
DNA gyrase inhibitors (fluoroquinolones)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Some translation inhibitors (e.g. tetracycline)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Moderately high temperature (44 °C)&lt;/strong&gt; clusters with:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
DNA-damaging antibiotics (nitrofurantoin)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Nucleotide biosynthesis inhibitors (trimethoprim)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Very high temperature (46 °C)&lt;/strong&gt; clusters with:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Aminoglycosides (cause mistranslation and protein misfolding)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This indicates that &lt;strong&gt;temperature and antibiotics perturb overlapping cellular subsystems&lt;/strong&gt; 41396_2018_article_241.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. Antibiotics activate temperature-stress gene programs&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Gene expression profiling shows:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Aminoglycosides induce &lt;strong&gt;heat-shock–like responses&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Tetracycline induces &lt;strong&gt;cold-shock–like responses&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Core chaperones (DnaK, GroEL, etc.) respond to both antibiotics and heat
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This confirms that &lt;strong&gt;antibiotics trigger ancestral temperature-stress pathways&lt;/strong&gt;, even when temperature itself is constant 41396_2018_article_241.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;What the paper specifically says about &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; encodes the &lt;strong&gt;β-subunit of RNA polymerase&lt;/strong&gt;, a central regulator of global transcription. Mutations in &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; are best known for conferring &lt;strong&gt;rifampicin resistance&lt;/strong&gt;, but this paper places &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; in a much broader evolutionary and physiological context.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations arise during heat adaptation (without antibiotics)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper builds directly on prior work (Rodríguez-Verdugo et al. 2013), where &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; evolved at &lt;strong&gt;42.2 °C&lt;/strong&gt; for ~2,000 generations &lt;strong&gt;without antibiotics&lt;/strong&gt; nonetheless became &lt;strong&gt;rifampicin-resistant&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The resistance was traced to &lt;strong&gt;mutations in &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This demonstrates that &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations can be selected purely by temperature stress&lt;/strong&gt;, not drug exposure 41396_2018_article_241.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key implication:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations are not “antibiotic-specific” adaptations—they are &lt;strong&gt;global stress-adaptation mutations&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;4. Heat-adapted strains with &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations show predictable cross-resistance&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this paper, the authors profile:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
10 independently heat-adapted strains (most carrying &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The ancestral strain
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Engineered &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutants
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They find that heat-adapted strains are:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;More resistant&lt;/strong&gt; to antibiotics that cluster with &lt;strong&gt;high temperature&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g. nitrofurantoin, trimethoprim)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;More sensitive&lt;/strong&gt; to antibiotics that cluster with &lt;strong&gt;low temperature&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g. clindamycin, erythromycin, tetracycline)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This matches the &lt;strong&gt;SIN predictions&lt;/strong&gt;, linking &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt;-associated heat adaptation to &lt;strong&gt;directional changes in antibiotic susceptibility&lt;/strong&gt; 41396_2018_article_241.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;5. &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations alone do NOT explain all resistance patterns&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A crucial nuance:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Isolated &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutants&lt;/strong&gt; do &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; fully recapitulate the resistance profiles of the evolved heat-adapted strains.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For some antibiotics (e.g. ERY, CLI), &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutants behave differently from fully evolved strains.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations are &lt;strong&gt;important but not sufficient&lt;/strong&gt;. Heat adaptation involves:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Additional compensatory or interacting mutations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
System-level transcriptional rewiring
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This underscores that &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; acts as a &lt;strong&gt;global regulatory pivot&lt;/strong&gt;, not a single-drug resistance switch 41396_2018_article_241.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;6. &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; links temperature stress to antibiotic resistance mechanistically&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Why &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; matters mechanistically:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;RNA polymerase controls:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Stress-response regulons
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Growth–stress trade-offs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Expression of chaperones, repair systems, and metabolic genes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutations in &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; alter:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Transcriptional fidelity
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Sigma-factor competition
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Stress tolerance vs growth optimization
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thus, &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations likely:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Improve transcriptional robustness at high temperature
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Incidentally buffer damage caused by DNA-damaging antibiotics
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Create vulnerabilities to “cold-like” translation inhibitors
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This positions &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; as a &lt;strong&gt;key evolutionary conduit&lt;/strong&gt; linking environmental stress to drug resistance.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Big-picture conclusion (with &lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; emphasized)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper argues that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Antibiotic resistance can evolve as a by-product of temperature adaptation&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; is a central genetic node enabling this cross-stress resistance&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Resistance is best understood as a &lt;strong&gt;systems-level stress response&lt;/strong&gt;, not just drug-target mutation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Environmental stress (like heat) can pre-adapt bacteria for resistance &lt;strong&gt;before antibiotics are encountered&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In short:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;rpoB&lt;/em&gt; mutations exemplify how ancient stress responses are repurposed to resist modern antibiotics.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
