    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_277">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-citation-items=&quot;%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FMVN2DQ72%22%5D%2C%22itemData%22%3A%7B%22id%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FMVN2DQ72%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22article-journal%22%2C%22abstract%22%3A%22Evolution%20can%20be%20contingent%20on%20history%2C%20but%20we%20do%20not%20yet%20have%20a%20clear%20understanding%20of%20the%20processes%20and%20dynamics%20that%20govern%20contingency.%20Here%2C%20we%20performed%20the%20second%20phase%20of%20a%20two-phase%20evolution%20experiment%20to%20investigate%20features%20of%20contingency.%20The%20first%20phase%20of%20the%20experiment%20was%20based%20on%20Escherichia%20coli%20clones%20that%20had%20evolved%20at%20the%20stressful%20temperature%20of%2042.2%20%C2%B0C.%20The%20Phase%201%20lines%20generally%20evolved%20through%20two%20adaptive%20pathways%3A%20mutations%20of%20rpoB%2C%20which%20encodes%20the%20beta%20subunit%20of%20RNA%20polymerase%2C%20or%20through%20rho%2C%20a%20transcriptional%20terminator.%20We%20hypothesized%20that%20epistatic%20interactions%20within%20the%20two%20pathways%20constrained%20their%20future%20adaptative%20potential%2C%20thus%20affecting%20patterns%20of%20historical%20contingency.%20Using%20ten%20different%20E.%20coli%20Founders%20representing%20both%20adaptive%20pathways%2C%20we%20performed%20a%20second%20phase%20of%20evolution%20at%2019.0%20%C2%B0C%20to%20investigate%20how%20prior%20genetic%20divergence%20or%20adaptive%20pathway%20(rpoB%20vs.%20rho)%20affects%20evolutionary%20outcomes.%20We%20found%20that%20phenotype%2C%20as%20measured%20by%20relative%20fitness%2C%20was%20contingent%20on%20founder%20genotypes%20and%20pathways.%20This%20finding%20extended%20to%20genotypes%2C%20because%20E.%20coli%20from%20different%20Phase%201%20histories%20evolved%20by%20adaptive%20mutations%20in%20distinct%20sets%20of%20genes.%20Our%20results%20suggest%20that%20evolution%20depends%20critically%20on%20genetic%20history%2C%20likely%20due%20to%20idiosyncratic%20epistatic%20interactions%20within%20and%20between%20evolutionary%20modules.%22%2C%22container-title%22%3A%22Molecular%20Biology%20and%20Evolution%22%2C%22DOI%22%3A%2210.1093%2Fmolbev%2Fmsad108%22%2C%22ISSN%22%3A%220737-4038%2C%201537-1719%22%2C%22issue%22%3A%225%22%2C%22language%22%3A%22en%22%2C%22license%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Fcreativecommons.org%2Flicenses%2Fby%2F4.0%2F%22%2C%22page%22%3A%22msad108%22%2C%22source%22%3A%22DOI.org%20(Crossref)%22%2C%22title%22%3A%22Phenotypic%20and%20Genotypic%20Adaptation%20of%20%3Ci%3EEscherichia%20coli%3C%2Fi%3E%20to%20Thermal%20Stress%20is%20Contingent%20on%20Genetic%20Background%22%2C%22URL%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Facademic.oup.com%2Fmbe%2Farticle%2Fdoi%2F10.1093%2Fmolbev%2Fmsad108%2F7151523%22%2C%22volume%22%3A%2240%22%2C%22author%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Batarseh%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Tiffany%20N%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Batarseh%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Sarah%20N%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Rodr%C3%ADguez-Verdugo%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Alejandra%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Gaut%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Brandon%20S%22%7D%5D%2C%22editor%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Hendrickson%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Heather%22%7D%5D%2C%22accessed%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222026%22%2C2%2C5%5D%5D%7D%2C%22issued%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222023%22%2C5%2C2%5D%5D%7D%7D%7D%5D&quot; data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F62V8JYX5%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%222%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A1%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B457.3358999999993%2C505.5474%2C544.956299999999%2C514.4094%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C493.5249%2C544.9637%2C502.3911%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C481.5097%2C544.9405499999991%2C490.37170000000003%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C469.5502%2C544.939499999999%2C478.41220000000004%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C457.52770000000004%2C544.9174499999997%2C466.3908%5D%2C%5B309.71220000000005%2C445.5063%2C544.9625999999992%2C454.36830000000003%5D%2C%5B309.71220000000005%2C433.5468%2C544.959449999999%2C442.40880000000004%5D%2C%5B309.71220000000005%2C421.52430000000004%2C544.9467999999999%2C430.39160000000004%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C409.5102%2C544.9394999999989%2C418.3722%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C397.5507%2C544.977299999999%2C406.41270000000003%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C385.5282%2C544.9552499999998%2C394.3912%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C373.511%2C544.9605%2C382.37300000000005%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C361.5483%2C544.9657499999992%2C370.4103%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C349.5258%2C544.9815%2C358.392%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C337.5117%2C544.9541999999993%2C346.37370000000004%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C325.5522%2C544.9405499999991%2C334.41420000000005%5D%2C%5B309.7101%2C313.2105%2C472.0999499999995%2C322.78650000000005%5D%2C%5B320.7088%2C301.5114%2C544.9772499999993%2C310.3734%5D%2C%5B309.7048%2C289.5519%2C544.9541499999991%2C298.4139%5D%2C%5B309.7048%2C277.5294%2C544.93525%2C286.3924%5D%2C%5B309.70480000000003%2C265.5079%2C450.44679999999937%2C274.36990000000003%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FMVN2DQ72%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%222%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“We focus on extreme temperatures for four reasons. First, temperature is a fundamental environmental property that affects physiological traits and often defines species’ distributions; hence, it often requires a complex evolutionary response (Somero 1978; Cooper et al. 2001). Second, temperature adaptation often, but not always, leads to trade-offs at other temperatures (Rodríguez-Verdugo et al. 2014), suggesting that contingency could be important in this system. Third, temperature is a topic with rich historical precedent in the experimental evolution literature. For example, Bennett and Lenski (1993) evolved E. coli at 20 °C, near the lower edge of the temperature niche, after first adapting them to the upper end of the temperature niche (42.2 °C). They found no convincing evidence of contingency at the phenotypic level, but their work was based on a relatively small number of samples (n = 12) and lacked genetic information. The fourth reason that we focus on temperature is that we can take advantage of a previous large-scale experiment. Tenaillon et al. (2012) evolved 115 lines from a single E. coli founder strain (REL1206) at 42.2 °C.”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FMVN2DQ72%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%222%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Batarseh et al., 2023, p. 2&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F62V8JYX5%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%222%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A1%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B339.5931%2C133.5324%2C544.9458%2C142.39650000000003%5D%2C%5B309.7038%2C121.51200000000003%2C367.9158%2C130.37400000000002%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FMVN2DQ72%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%222%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“rpoB clones tended to have mutations in rod, ILV, and RSS genes”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FMVN2DQ72%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%222%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Batarseh et al., 2023, p. 2&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F62V8JYX5%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%2211%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A10%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B75.9108%2C469.5482%2C285.5643%2C478.41020000000003%5D%2C%5B50.34329999999997%2C457.52570000000003%2C285.5538%2C466.39500000000004%5D%2C%5B50.34329999999997%2C445.5105%2C285.5853%2C454.37250000000006%5D%2C%5B50.332800000000006%2C433.551%2C285.58004999999997%2C442.413%5D%2C%5B50.33280000000002%2C421.5285%2C285.57165000000015%2C430.39050000000003%5D%2C%5B50.33280000000002%2C409.50600000000003%2C285.59265%2C418.36800000000005%5D%2C%5B50.33280000000002%2C397.54650000000004%2C285.58215000000007%2C406.40850000000006%5D%2C%5B50.33280000000002%2C385.52400000000006%2C285.5957499999999%2C394.3912%5D%2C%5B50.34219999999999%2C373.5067%2C285.5873499999996%2C382.36870000000005%5D%2C%5B50.34219999999999%2C361.54720000000003%2C285.60309999999964%2C370.40920000000006%5D%2C%5B50.34219999999999%2C349.52470000000005%2C285.6126%2C358.392%5D%2C%5B50.3433%2C337.5117%2C285.5673999999999%2C346.37370000000004%5D%2C%5B50.34009999999998%2C325.549%2C285.5716%2C334.41420000000005%5D%2C%5B50.3433%2C313.5308%2C285.6126%2C322.3928%5D%2C%5B50.3433%2C301.5114%2C285.6073499999997%2C310.3734%5D%2C%5B50.3433%2C289.5519%2C261.3302999999997%2C298.4139%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FMVN2DQ72%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%2211%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“At least three of the five enriched regions in rpoB lineages are related to transcriptional function: rpoC, rho, and hepA (table 3). rpoC codes for the beta-prime subunit of RNA polymerase (Trinh et al. 2006; Conrad et al. 2010); the RHO protein terminates RNA polymerase activity; and hepA (which also known as rapA) encodes a transcription factor with ATPase activity that it is an RNA polymerase associated protein (Sukhodolets et al. 2001). Previous work has shown that modifying RNA polymerase is a key feature of adaptation to thermal stress but also that this is a blunt instrument that may cause more phenotypic changes (as measured by gene expression; RodriguezVerdugo et al. 2016) than may be necessary to achieve fitness gains. If true, it is reasonable to speculate that adaptation to 19.0 °C from 42.2 °C includes further tuning of RNA polymerase, as reflected by an enrichment of genes r”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FMVN2DQ72%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%2211%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Batarseh et al., 2023, p. 11&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;highlight&quot; data-annotation=&quot;%7B%22attachmentURI%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2F62V8JYX5%22%2C%22pageLabel%22%3A%2211%22%2C%22position%22%3A%7B%22pageIndex%22%3A10%2C%22rects%22%3A%5B%5B70.07595%2C253.55270000000002%2C285.55800000000005%2C262.41470000000004%5D%2C%5B50.3475%2C241.527%2C285.5548500000001%2C250.3922%5D%2C%5B50.332800000000006%2C229.50449999999998%2C285.5811%2C238.36649999999997%5D%2C%5B50.33279999999999%2C217.545%2C285.60420000000016%2C226.4123%5D%2C%5B50.3433%2C205.52779999999998%2C285.5496%2C214.38979999999998%5D%2C%5B50.3433%2C193.50529999999998%2C129.49965%2C202.3746%5D%5D%7D%2C%22citationItem%22%3A%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FMVN2DQ72%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%2211%22%7D%7D&quot;&gt;“First, two enriched regions within rpoB populations (valY and lysV; table 3) encode tRNA synthetases (Andersen et al. 1997; Ruan et al. 2011; Agrawal et al. 2014), suggesting that one additional or alternative route to adaptation is through modifications of translational speed or dynamics.”&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F19606070%2Fitems%2FMVN2DQ72%22%5D%2C%22locator%22%3A%2211%22%7D%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;(&lt;span class=&quot;citation-item&quot;&gt;Batarseh et al., 2023, p. 11&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
