        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_321"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_322"/>
        <dc:title>Epistasis and pleiotropy‐induced variation for plant breeding</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Epistasis refers to nonallelic interaction between genes that cause bias in estimates of genetic parameters for a phenotype with interactions of two or more genes affecting the same trait. Partitioning of epistatic effects allows true estimation of the genetic parameters affecting phenotypes. Multigenic variation plays a central role in the evolution of complex characteristics, among which pleiotropy, where a single gene affects several phenotypic characters, has a large inﬂuence. While pleiotropic interactions provide functional speciﬁcity, they increase the challenge of gene discovery and functional analysis. Overcoming pleiotropy-based phenotypic trade-offs offers potential for assisting breeding for complex traits. Modelling higher order nonallelic epistatic interaction, pleiotropy and non-pleiotropy-induced variation, and genotype 9 environment interaction in genomic selection may provide new paths to increase the productivity and stress tolerance for next generation of crop cultivars. Advances in statistical models, software and algorithm developments, and genomic research have facilitated dissecting the nature and extent of pleiotropy and epistasis. We overview emerging approaches to exploit positive (and avoid negative) epistatic and pleiotropic interactions in a plant breeding context, including developing avenues of artiﬁcial intelligence, novel exploitation of large-scale genomics and phenomics data, and involvement of genes with minor effects to analyse epistatic interactions and pleiotropic quantitative trait loci, including missing heritability.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>10/2024</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/pbi.14405</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-19 00:17:24</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>2788-2807</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_321">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;h2&gt;1. What the paper says about &lt;strong&gt;epistasis&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Core argument&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper treats epistasis as &lt;strong&gt;ubiquitous and biologically fundamental&lt;/strong&gt;, not a statistical nuisance. The authors argue that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Epistasis (non-additive gene–gene interaction) is &lt;strong&gt;widespread in complex agronomic traits&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ignoring epistasis leads directly to:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;biased estimates of genetic parameters&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;missing heritability&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;reduced prediction accuracy in genomic selection&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Epistasis contributes strongly to:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
heterosis
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
background-dependent allele effects
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
genotype × environment interaction (G×E)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They explicitly reject the idea that additive models are “good enough” in principle, even if they sometimes work pragmatically PBI-22-2788.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Important nuance&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors emphasize that &lt;strong&gt;epistasis is often conditional&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
on genetic background
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
on environment
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
on regulatory context
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This means the &lt;em&gt;same allele&lt;/em&gt; can be beneficial in one background and deleterious in another—an issue that standard GS models cannot capture.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;2. What the paper says about &lt;strong&gt;pleiotropy&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Core argument&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Pleiotropy is framed as a &lt;strong&gt;double-edged sword&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Positive pleiotropy&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;→ one gene improves multiple traits (e.g. yield + stress tolerance)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Antagonistic pleiotropy&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;→ improvement in one trait causes decline in another (classic trade-offs)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors stress that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Most genes show &lt;strong&gt;low-degree pleiotropy&lt;/strong&gt; (L-shaped distribution)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A small subset of genes (often transcription factors) show &lt;strong&gt;multi-role pleiotropy&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Pleiotropy is a &lt;strong&gt;major driver of genetic correlations&lt;/strong&gt; among traits
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Crucially, they argue that &lt;strong&gt;many genetic correlations assumed to be pleiotropic are actually due to tight linkage&lt;/strong&gt;, which has different implications for selection PBI-22-2788.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;3. How epistasis affects &lt;strong&gt;genomic selection&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key claim&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ignoring epistasis limits the ceiling of GS accuracy&lt;/strong&gt;, especially for complex traits.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper argues that standard GS:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
assumes additivity
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
averages allele effects across backgrounds
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;therefore &lt;strong&gt;systematically underperforms&lt;/strong&gt; when:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
epistatic interactions are strong
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
minor-effect loci interact
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
heterosis or stability is the breeding target
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They cite empirical evidence where:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
incorporating epistatic QTL or interaction terms improves prediction
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
GS accuracy improves when epistatic loci are included as covariates
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;But the authors are realistic&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They acknowledge:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
exhaustive epistasis modelling is computationally infeasible
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
high-order epistasis is statistically underpowered
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
most epistatic effects are small and unstable across environments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So they do &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; argue for full mechanistic epistasis modelling in GS, but for &lt;strong&gt;strategic inclusion&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g. interaction-aware ML, network-based priors).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;4. How pleiotropy affects &lt;strong&gt;genomic selection&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Trait correlations are the bottleneck&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper emphasizes that pleiotropy:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
constrains independent trait optimization
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
explains why improving yield often worsens quality or stress tolerance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
creates &lt;strong&gt;hidden selection trade-offs&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For GS, this means:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
multi-trait GS is essential
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
single-trait GS can inadvertently select against correlated traits
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
pleiotropic loci can dominate prediction even if they are not optimal targets
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors argue that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;GS models should explicitly distinguish:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
pleiotropic vs non-pleiotropic QTL
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
positive vs antagonistic pleiotropy
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
otherwise, GS risks &lt;strong&gt;reinforcing undesirable correlations&lt;/strong&gt; PBI-22-2788.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;5. Where the paper is &lt;strong&gt;strong&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Conceptual strengths&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Clear separation of &lt;strong&gt;biological epistasis&lt;/strong&gt; vs &lt;strong&gt;statistical epistasis&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Strong synthesis across crops and trait classes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Explicit link between:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
epistasis → missing heritability → GS bias
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Good coverage of emerging tools (ML, networks, multi-trait GWAS)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Strategic insight&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper is especially strong in arguing that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The goal is not to model everything, but to avoid systematically wrong assumptions.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a mature stance that avoids the “epistasis everywhere” trap.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;6. Where the paper is &lt;strong&gt;weak or over-optimistic&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Limited empirical payoff&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While many tools are reviewed, the paper:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
rarely quantifies &lt;strong&gt;how much GS accuracy actually improves&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
often cites proof-of-concept rather than breeding-program-scale gains
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Tool-driven optimism&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There is a recurring assumption that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;better models + more data = better prediction&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
epistasis is often &lt;strong&gt;environment-specific&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
pleiotropic effects shift across populations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ML models risk being &lt;strong&gt;predictive but biologically opaque&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This weakens the claim that epistasis-aware GS is broadly deployable &lt;em&gt;now&lt;/em&gt;, rather than aspirational.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;7. Big picture takeaway&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;The paper’s central message&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Epistasis and pleiotropy are not exceptions—they are core features of complex traits—and genomic selection that ignores them will eventually plateau.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;But the implied constraint&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Full modelling is unrealistic
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Selective, biologically informed integration is the only viable path
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In other words, the paper argues for &lt;strong&gt;evolution-aware genomic selection&lt;/strong&gt;, not brute-force interaction modelling.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
