        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_332"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_333"/>
        <dc:title>Cost of resistance: an unreasonably expensive concept</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>The concept of “cost of resistance” has been very important for decades, for fundamental reasons (theory of adaptation), with a wide range of applications for the genetics and genomics of resistance: resistance to antibiotics, insecticide, herbicide, fungicides; resistance to chemotherapy in cancer research; coevolution between all kinds of parasites and their hosts. This paper reviews this history, including latest developments, shows the interest of the idea but also challenges the usefulness and limits of this widely used concept, based on the most recent development of adaptation theory.  It explains how the concept can be flawed and how this can impede research efforts in the field of resistance at large, including all applied aspects. In particular, it would be clearer to simply measure the fitness effects of mutations across environments and to better distinguish those effects from ‘pleiotropic effects’ of those mutations. Overall, we show how to correct the concept, and how this correction helps to better understand the wealth of data that has accumulated in recent years. The main points are:
            1. The concept of «cost of resistance» needs to be carefully used, to avoid misconceptions, false paradox and flawed applications. The recent developments in adaptation theory are useful to clarify this.
            2. “Cost of resistance” and pleiotropy have to be distinguished. More than one trait is required to discuss pleiotropy. Resistance evolution must at least involve the modification of one trait. If there is an irreducible trade-off on that trait between environments with and without drug, it creates a fitness effect that is not due to pleiotropy. Pleiotropic effects can, but need not, occur in addition.
            3. “Cost of resistance” must depend on the pair of environments considered with and without drug. Hence, there are as many measures of cost as there are environments without drug. If the focal genotype is not well adapted to one focal environment, it is relatively easy to observe “negative” costs of resistance. There is nothing surprising about this, and it does not indicate an absence of trade-off.
            4. Environments with drug can differ according to the dose. It may be more informative to measure the possible trade-offs among all doses than to focus exclusively on the fitness contrast between the presence and the absence of drug.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2018-12-31</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Cost of resistance</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://rethinkingecology.pensoft.net/article/31992/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-02-24 06:07:50</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>51-70</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_332">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;The paper argues that the widely used concept of the &lt;strong&gt;“cost of resistance”&lt;/strong&gt; in evolutionary biology is often &lt;strong&gt;misleading, oversimplified, and sometimes incorrect&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Instead of thinking of resistance mutations as having a fixed “benefit” (with drug) and “cost” (without drug), the authors argue that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fitness effects of resistance mutations are context-dependent across environments and traits, not intrinsic properties of the mutation itself.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They propose that researchers should focus on &lt;strong&gt;fitness across environments&lt;/strong&gt; rather than relying on the simplistic idea of a single “cost.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🎯 &lt;strong&gt;What “Cost of Resistance” Means Traditionally&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In fields like antibiotic resistance, pesticide resistance, or cancer therapy:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A mutation helps survival when the drug is present → &lt;strong&gt;benefit&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The same mutation reduces fitness when the drug is absent → &lt;strong&gt;cost&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This idea is central to many strategies (e.g., stopping drug use to let resistance decline).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;⚠️ &lt;strong&gt;Why the Authors Say This Concept Is Problematic&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1) Fitness effects depend on environment (not intrinsic to the mutation)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A mutation is not inherently:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
beneficial
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
harmful
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
neutral
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It depends on ecological context, genetic background, and drug exposure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 The same mutation can be advantageous in one environment and disadvantageous — or even advantageous — in another.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2) “Cost” is not a single value — it varies across environments&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There is not one drug-free environment. There are many.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Therefore:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;There is not “a” cost of resistance — there are many possible costs.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In some environments without the drug:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Resistance mutation may be harmful
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Neutral
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Beneficial
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So the idea of a universal cost is flawed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3) “Negative cost” is possible — resistance can be beneficial even without the drug&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Sometimes resistant organisms outperform wild types even when the drug is absent.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This happens when the wild type is not optimally adapted to that environment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 This does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; mean resistance has no trade-off — just that the comparison reference is misleading.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;4) Cost ≠ Pleiotropy&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A major clarification of the paper:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;❌ Common assumption:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cost comes from pleiotropic side-effects of resistance mutations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;✅ Authors’ argument:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These are &lt;strong&gt;different phenomena&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pleiotropy:&lt;/strong&gt; Mutation affects multiple traits
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cost of resistance:&lt;/strong&gt; Fitness change across environments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Even if pleiotropic effects are compensated, a trade-off between environments can still remain.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;5) Trade-offs between environments are unavoidable&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Resistance usually requires shifting toward a phenotype optimal in the drug environment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That shift moves the organism away from the optimum in drug-free conditions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is an inherent trade-off — not necessarily due to pleiotropy.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;6) Drug environments are not binary (present vs absent)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Drug concentration varies continuously in real life.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Therefore:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Studying only “drug vs no drug” oversimplifies reality.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Different doses may produce different fitness landscapes and trade-offs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;7) The concept introduces a misleading asymmetry&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Researchers talk about:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
cost of resistance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;…but not:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
cost of susceptibility in drug environments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Both are simply context-dependent fitness differences.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🧩 Conceptual Model Used&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors use a &lt;strong&gt;fitness landscape / phenotypic optimum model&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Each environment has its own optimal phenotype
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Resistance mutations move organisms toward the drug-environment optimum
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Distance from the optimum determines fitness
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This framework shows why trade-offs arise naturally without invoking “cost” as a special property.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🧪 Why This Matters (Practical Implications)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Misusing the concept can:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mislead resistance management strategies
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Create false expectations about reversing resistance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Obscure real evolutionary dynamics
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Confuse interpretation of experiments
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For example, stopping antibiotic use will not necessarily eliminate resistance if resistant strains are also fit without the drug.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;✅ What the Authors Recommend Instead&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They propose abandoning or reformulating the concept and focusing on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;👉 Measure fitness directly across environments&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Instead of asking:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“What is the cost of resistance?”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ask:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“What are the fitness effects of this mutation in each environment?”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Also distinguish:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Environmental trade-offs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Pleiotropic effects
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Genetic background effects
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Dose-dependent responses
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;📌 Final Take-Home Message&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Resistance mutations do not carry an intrinsic “cost.”&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Fitness consequences emerge from the interaction between genotype, environment, and traits.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The traditional cost-of-resistance framework is an oversimplified shortcut that can obscure more than it explains.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you want, I can also:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;✅ Relate this paper directly to antibiotic resistance in &lt;em&gt;E. coli&lt;/em&gt; (relevant to your previous questions)&lt;br&gt;✅ Explain how it connects to rpoB mutations and compensatory evolution&lt;br&gt;✅ Summarize it in “exam-ready” notes&lt;br&gt;✅ Extract key quotes or figures&lt;br&gt;✅ Compare it to other resistance-evolution theories&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Just say what you need.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;give me a more comprehensive overview of cost does not equal Pleiotropy section and break down the concepts&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Here is a &lt;strong&gt;deep, concept-level breakdown&lt;/strong&gt; of the section &lt;strong&gt;“Cost does not equal pleiotropy”&lt;/strong&gt; from the paper by Thomas Lenormand and colleagues.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is one of the most important — and most misunderstood — parts of the paper.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🧠 Core Claim&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The fitness cost of resistance is NOT the same thing as pleiotropic effects of a resistance mutation.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Researchers often treat them as interchangeable, but the authors argue they are fundamentally different biological phenomena.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🧩 First: What is Pleiotropy?&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;✅ Definition&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pleiotropy = one mutation affects multiple traits&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Example:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A mutation that confers antibiotic resistance might also change:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Growth rate
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Metabolism
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Cell wall structure
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Stress tolerance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Virulence
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Resource use
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These extra effects are &lt;strong&gt;pleiotropic effects&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;⚖️ Second: What is a “Cost of Resistance”?&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Traditionally:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reduced fitness of resistant organisms in environments without the drug.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Example:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Resistant bacteria grow slower than wild-type bacteria when antibiotics are absent.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;❌ Why Scientists Often Conflate Them&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Common assumption:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Resistance causes harmful side effects → those side effects reduce fitness → therefore cost = pleiotropy.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper says this logic is flawed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🔍 The Authors’ Key Insight&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🧭 Fitness cost arises from &lt;strong&gt;environmental trade-offs&lt;/strong&gt;, not necessarily from pleiotropic damage.&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A mutation can reduce fitness in a drug-free environment &lt;strong&gt;even if it has no harmful side effects at all.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🏔️ The Fitness Optimum Framework (Critical Concept)&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper uses a geometric model of adaptation:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Each environment has an optimal phenotype
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Organisms closer to that optimum have higher fitness
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutations move organisms in phenotype space
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🎯 Two different optima&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Imagine:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Environment A: no drug
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Environment B: drug present
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Each has a different optimal phenotype.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A resistance mutation moves the organism toward the optimum for Environment B.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;➡️ That movement usually moves it away from the optimum for Environment A.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;⚠️ Crucially:&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This trade-off happens even if the mutation only changes ONE trait.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;No pleiotropy required.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🧪 Example Without Pleiotropy&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Suppose resistance is achieved by changing only:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Binding affinity of a protein to an antibiotic target&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That change might:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Improve survival under drug exposure
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Reduce efficiency of the protein’s normal function
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But this reduction is not a separate side effect — it is the same trait being optimized differently in two environments.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🔄 Why This Is Not Pleiotropy&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Pleiotropy requires:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Effects on multiple distinct traits&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But here:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 The same trait has different optimal values in different environments&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So the fitness loss is due to &lt;strong&gt;environmental mismatch&lt;/strong&gt;, not extra trait damage.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;📌 Authors’ Key Distinction&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;1) Trade-off across environments&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Same trait
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Different optimal values
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
No additional biological side effects required
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;2) Pleiotropic effects&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mutation affects multiple independent traits
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Some may be harmful
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
These can also reduce fitness
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🧬 Compensation Does NOT Remove Trade-offs&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Another critical point:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Scientists often observe compensatory evolution — secondary mutations that restore fitness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Many interpret this as:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Removing pleiotropic costs&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But the authors warn:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Even if pleiotropic side effects are fully compensated…&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;➡️ The environmental trade-off can still remain.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Why?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Because the organism cannot simultaneously be perfectly adapted to both environments if their optima differ.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🧠 Why This Matters for Resistance Evolution&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Understanding this distinction changes how we interpret:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Persistence of resistance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Reversibility of resistance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Management strategies
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Evolutionary predictions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;⚠️ Misleading Consequence of Confusion&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If cost = pleiotropy, one might assume:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Fix the side effects → resistance becomes neutral → resistance disappears without drug&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But if cost comes from environmental trade-offs:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 Resistance may persist even after compensation&lt;br&gt;👉 Removing the drug may NOT eliminate resistant strains&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This has major implications for antibiotic policy.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🔬 Dose Dependence Adds Another Layer&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Different drug concentrations shift the optimal phenotype continuously.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Therefore:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Resistance mutation may be optimal at high doses
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Suboptimal at low doses
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Neutral at intermediate levels
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Again, not necessarily pleiotropic.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;📉 When Pleiotropy DOES Matter&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors do not say pleiotropy is irrelevant.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They say:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;👉 It is one possible contributor to fitness costs — but not the defining mechanism.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Costs may result from:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Pure environmental trade-offs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Pleiotropic side effects
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Genetic background interactions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Ecological factors
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🧩 Visual Intuition&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Think of adaptation like tuning a machine for two different tasks:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Tune for task A → worse at task B
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Tune for task B → worse at task A
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;No parts are “broken.”&lt;br&gt;The settings just cannot be optimal for both simultaneously.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;🧾 Bottom Line of the Section&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;❌ Incorrect idea:&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cost of resistance = harmful side effects of resistance mutations&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;✅ Correct interpretation:&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cost of resistance = reduced fitness due to mismatch with the optimal phenotype in another environment&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Pleiotropy may contribute — but is neither necessary nor sufficient.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;⭐ One-Sentence Summary&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Resistance costs arise primarily from evolutionary trade-offs between environments, not from pleiotropic damage caused by resistance mutations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
